          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[00:45] Recall:</strong> Which wing of the King David Hotel was destroyed, and what two British administrative bodies were headquartered there?</li><li><strong>[01:50] Explain:</strong> Explain why the bombing of the King David Hotel by Menachem Begin's Irgun convinced British ministers that maintaining peacekeeping forces in Mandatory Palestine was politically untenable.</li><li><strong>[02:30] Challenge:</strong> Analyze the newsreel narrator's choice of language and dramatic framing. How does comparing the wreckage to 'the Blitz' shape British public perceptions of Zionist insurgents and justify British withdrawal?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> The south-west wing was destroyed, which housed both the central Secretariat of the Government of Palestine and the Headquarters of British Forces in Palestine.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> The bombing killed 91 people (British, Arab, and Jewish civil servants and soldiers) within Britain's most heavily guarded military headquarters. Following six years of WWII sacrifice, the British public and Parliament refused to endure further casualties in an intractable colonial conflict, making continued occupation politically and economically unsustainable.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> By invoking the 'Blitz' and labeling insurgents a 'gang of desperados' committing a 'dastardly crime', the newsreel presents Britain exclusively as an innocent, victimized peacekeeper. This framed Zionist insurgent tactics as unprovoked criminal terrorism rather than a political response to British immigration quotas, mobilizing British domestic opinion to demand the immediate end of the Mandate.</li></ol>"
