          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[02:15] Recall:</strong> In 1937, what percentage of land did the Peel Commission propose allocating to the Arab and Jewish populations?</li><li><strong>[03:40] Explain:</strong> Explain why David Ben-Gurion and the Jewish Agency accepted UN Resolution 181 despite being allocated non-contiguous territory and losing direct sovereign control over Jerusalem.</li><li><strong>[05:10] Challenge:</strong> Analyze how the presenter explains the Palestinian Arab rejection of partition. Does the video provide sufficient historical empathy for Arab demographic grievances regarding land ownership?</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> The 1937 Peel Commission proposed allocating roughly 75% of the land to the Arab population and 20–25% to the Jewish population.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> After the murder of six million European Jews in the Holocaust and with hundreds of thousands of displaced persons stranded in European refugee camps, sovereign statehood was an existential necessity. Ben-Gurion recognized that international legal recognition of a sovereign state outweighed painful territorial deficiencies (e.g. non-contiguous borders, international status for Jerusalem, and vast tracts of Negev desert).</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> The presenter acknowledges that Arabs constituted two-thirds of the population and had valid complaints about receiving less fertile land without their consent. However, by framing Arab opposition primarily as outright rejection rather than a defense of basic democratic self-determination, the narrative somewhat downplays the profound demographic injustice felt by indigenous Palestinian communities.</li></ol>"
