          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> Palestinian Arabs represented approximately two-thirds (roughly 67%) of the total population, numbering over 1.2 million compared to approximately 600,000 Jews.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Arab leaders argued that partition violated basic democratic principles of majority rule and self-determination. Allocating 55% of Palestine to the Jewish one-third—while trapping nearly 400,000 Arab villagers inside the proposed Jewish state without their democratic consent—was seen as an unjust colonial imposition orchestrated by Western powers.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Demographic and land tenure records provide objective statistical verification of who inhabited and farmed the land. Unlike emotional political rhetoric claiming either exclusive biblical title or total emptiness ('a land without a people'), census data proves that partition disrupted established agrarian communities, explaining why compromise was rejected on principle.</li></ol>"
