          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[01:30] Recall:</strong> Which US President issued the economic ultimatum to Britain, and what global financial institution did the US block from assisting London?</li><li><strong>[03:45] Explain:</strong> Explain why the United States adamantly opposed the Anglo-French invasion of Egypt, despite Britain being America's closest NATO ally.</li><li><strong>[05:20] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate why historians consider the 1956 Suez Crisis the defining event that ended Britain and France's status as independent global superpowers.</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> President Dwight D. Eisenhower; the US blocked emergency loans from the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and threatened to sell off US holdings of British sterling.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Eisenhower was furious that Britain and France had acted without consulting Washington, distracting the world from the simultaneous Soviet invasion of Hungary. Furthermore, Washington feared that supporting European colonial gunboat diplomacy would alienate the newly independent post-colonial nations of Africa and Asia, driving the entire Arab world directly into the Soviet sphere of influence.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> When threatened with financial ruin by Washington, Britain was forced into an immediate, humiliating ceasefire and unconditional evacuation. The crisis proved conclusively that European imperial powers could no longer wage major military operations overseas without American consent, transferring geopolitical primacy entirely to the two nuclear superpowers: the USA and USSR.</li></ol>"
