          "teacher_guidance": "Superb BBC dramatized documentary examining the hijackings of Pan Am, TWA, and Swissair flights, hostage negotiations, and the regional fallout. Recommended Watch Window: 05:00–14:00 • Lesson Phase: High-Attainer Stretch & Asymmetric Warfare. Tone Alert: High-impact BBC dramatization of the international hijackings and secret negotiations.",
          "viewing_task": "<ol><li><strong>[06:10] Recall:</strong> Which Palestinian female militant gained international notoriety for leading the hijackings of TWA Flight 840 (1969) and El Al Flight 219 (1970)?</li><li><strong>[09:20] Explain:</strong> Why did Palestinian factions like the PFLP conclude that skyjackings and attacks on Western civilian targets were far more effective than conventional cross-border guerrilla warfare?</li><li><strong>[12:30] Challenge:</strong> Evaluate how airline hijackings successfully forced the Palestinian refugee plight into global consciousness while simultaneously alienating Western public opinion and provoking host-state crackdowns.</li></ol>",
          "model_answer": "<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> Leila Khaled (of the Popular Front for the Liberation of Palestine).</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> Regular Arab armies had failed completely in 1948 and 1967. By seizing international commercial airliners, a tiny band of militants could paralyze Western governments, hold hundreds of foreign citizens hostage, and force world leaders to negotiate directly with them, neutralizing Israel's overwhelming conventional military advantage.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Hijackings put the word 'Palestinian' on the front page of every global newspaper, transforming refugees into recognized political actors. However, it severely damaged their moral legitimacy in the West, caused Western nations to designate Palestinian groups as terrorist organizations, and prompted King Hussein to violently expel them from Jordan to prevent foreign military intervention.</li></ol>"
