            "model": "The Oslo I Accord was of decisive historical importance because it shattered decades of mutual denial and established the unprecedented principle of official mutual recognition between Israel and the Palestine Liberation Organization (PLO). In the Letters of Mutual Recognition exchanged in September 1993, Yitzhak Rabin formally recognised the PLO as the legitimate representative of the Palestinian people, while Yasser Arafat officially recognised Israel’s right to exist in peace and security and unequivocally renounced all forms of terrorism. This historic breakthrough ended 45 years of non-recognition, transformed former mortal enemies into diplomatic negotiating partners, and paved the way for the dramatic handshake on the White House lawn between Rabin and Arafat on 13 September 1993.<br><br>Furthermore, the Accord was of critical importance because it established the framework for Palestinian self-government through the creation of the Palestinian National Authority (PNA). Under the \"Gaza-Jericho First\" formula agreed in the 1994 Cairo Agreement, Israeli troops redeployed out of populated Palestinian cities, enabling Arafat to return to Palestinian territory to head the newly formed PNA administration. However, the agreement was also important because its fatal ambiguities—deferring final status issues such as Jerusalem, Jewish settlements, and refugee right of return for five years—provoked violent opposition from extremists on both sides. Hamas and Palestinian Islamic Jihad launched deadly suicide bombings to derail the peace, while right-wing Israeli nationalists denounced Rabin as a traitor, creating the political climate that led directly to Rabin’s assassination in November 1995.",
